    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/949/cw/en/job/589103/research-fellow": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Research Fellow Apply now Job no: 589103 Work type: Full-time Location: Melbourne - Burwood Categories: Research Based at our Melbourne, Burwood Campus + hybrid work arrangements Full-time and fixed-term until June 2030 Level B $117,684 - $139,191 + 17% Superannuation Why work with us? At Deakin you will have access to benefits such as a variety of leave options including generous parental leave and the ability to purchase additional leave; flexible working arrangements to help manage your work-life balance; ongoing learning and development opportunities to grow your career; an inclusive and supportive culture and environment to work in, both online and on campus. As a Research Fellow, you will: Initiate, design and conduct productive, high-quality research, scholarship and creative activities that support the objectives and guiding principles of The Evidence Exchange. Conduct research and research translation activities centred on innovative ways to change economic and commercial systems to better promote health and equity, with a focus on novel corporate governance mechanisms and ways to foster and scale up alternate forms of businesses. Support, and may lead, applications for research and creative activities, including applications for external competitive funding and translation activities. Build a national reputation, based on a growing, focused body of work recognised for quality, excellence and impact and a growing track record of timely delivery of outcomes. Initiate, design, conduct and may lead intra- and inter-disciplinary collaborations and partnerships, to enable major breakthroughs in knowledge and understanding, with a focus on solutions to complex problems. To be successful, you’ll have: PhD in a relevant discipline and/or other relevant qualifications and experience. Emerging reputation in research and scholarship through publications. Success in obtaining external research funding. Excellent interpersonal skills and a proven ability to establish good working relationships with colleagues and collaborators from multiple disciplines. Here's how to apply: Please submit your updated resume (max 3 pages) and a short cover letter (max 2 pages). The cover letter should outline your interest in the position, your ideas for particular areas of research and research translation aligned with the specified focus areas of the position, and your suitability for the role, taking into account the items listed under ‘Qualifications and experience’ in the Position Description. For a confidential discussion regarding this role, please contact Gary Sacks, Professor of Public Health Policy via gary.sacks@deakin.edu.au . For a copy of the position description, please see below: PD_Research Fellow - 06102026 (1).pdf Applications for this position close at 11.55pm on the 5 th of November. Are You Ready? Deakin is a Victorian university with a global impact. We are an agile, dynamic, and innovative university committed to making a positive impact through our excellence in education, research and innovation and the contributions we make to the wider community. We understand that our reputation has been built on the dedication and expertise of our staff and we offer a dynamic and diverse working environment with opportunities to grow and develop careers. We believe that a progressive, thriving culture will ensure that people choose to come, and stay at Deakin and contribute to our ongoing success. We value diversity and aim to build an inclusive environment that champions, embraces and respects differences. We support and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and people of all abilities, cultures, sexual orientation, and genders. Pre-employment safety and suitability checks: In accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence, appointment to this role is subject to successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, including Working With Children Checks and candidate gender-based violence declarations. Candidates may be asked to declare whether they have been investigated for an allegation of Gender‑based Violence, or determined to have engaged in conduct that constitutes Gender‑based Violence during the course of their previous employment, or otherwise in a legal process. A declaration is not required from individuals who have experienced gender‑based violence. A ‘Yes’ response does not automatically exclude a candidate from employment, and any information provided will be treated confidentially and considered only for relevance to the role and the University’s safety obligations. Advertised: 08 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/949/cw/en/job/589625/lead-cyber-security-threat-intelligence": {
      "title": "Lead, Cyber Security Threat Intelligence",
      "description": "Lead, Cyber Security Threat Intelligence Apply now Job no: 589625 Work type: Full-time Location: Flexible Categories: Information Technology Flexible location based at Melbourne, Burwood or Geelong, Waterfront Campus + hybrid work arrangements Full-time and fixed term for 12 months $143,627 - $152,229 (HEW 9) + 17% Superannuation About the team and role: Deakin remains a leader in cyber security and quality assurance – empowering the university to confidently navigate challenges and ensure Deakin’s organisational resilience. The team safeguards Deakin’s digital assets by detecting and responding to threats, managing risks, ensuring cyber awareness and embedding a zero-trust security strategy. Through initiatives such as Project Lighthouse, we enhance Deakin’s cyber security maturity, ensuring a proactive and resilient approach to evolving cyber threats. The Lead, Cyber Security Threat Intelligence leads the University’s threat intelligence function and contributes to planning, prioritisation and decision-making aligned to University priorities. The role integrates threat intelligence, cyber risk information, stakeholder insight and operational requirements to strengthen the University's tactical and operational cyber threat intelligence capability, inform proactive risk management and support effective cyber security outcomes across a complex and evolving threat landscape. As a Lead, you will: Lead and mature the tactical and operational cyber threat intelligence capability Oversee Threat Intelligence Platform (TIP) and IOC integration with SOC/SIEM Act as the primary intelligence liaison to security operations and security engineering Lead threat modelling workshops and support exercise/tabletop scenarios across technology domains Provide intelligence support to incident response and major cyber events To be successful, you’ll have: Postgraduate qualifications and extensive relevant experience, or an equivalent combination of education, training and expertise. Demonstrated success leading programs, functions or major initiatives in complex environments. Strong analytical, critical thinking and problem-solving skills, with the ability to drive informed decision-making. Proven ability to influence stakeholders, build productive relationships and communicate effectively at all levels. Sound understanding of governance and policy frameworks, with experience in threat detection tools and techniques. Here's how to apply: Only people with the right to work in Australia may apply for this position. Please submit your updated resume and a short cover letter outlining your skills and experience by clicking ‘Apply Now’. For a confidential discussion regarding this role, please contact Ronnie Islam (Director, Cyber Security Operations) on ronnie.islam@deakin.edu.au or Nupur Chadha (Senior Director, Resilience & Cyber Security) on nupur.chadha@deakin.edu.au For a copy of the position description, please see below: Lead, Cyber Security Threat Intelligence - HEW 9_PD.pdf Applications for this position close on Thursday, 22 October 2026 at 11:55pm. Make an Impact with Us Together we aim to take Deakin forward, strengthening our progressive University through the digital work we do. Whether it's empowering students, enabling teaching staff, or supporting researchers, the work you do here matters. Your achievements contribute to making a positive difference in the lives of the people and communities we serve. So that you can focus on making a positive impact, Deakin Digital Services lays the foundations for you to be your best self, grow and evolve and join forces with exceptional people. Be Your Best Self As a unique individual we understand that showing up as the best version of yourself takes commitment to your personal wellbeing. That’s why we foster a supportive environment where you can manage your work to balance your productivity, engagement and wellbeing. Our approach to flexible working empowers you to achieve your best. Grow & Evolve Being in the higher education sector, ours is an environment of knowledge sharing and seeking. With us you can grow your capability through formal and informal learning and continuously improve, deepen and broaden your expertise. With many digital roles and teams under the one umbrella, there’s opportunity to expand your skillset, embrace new challenges and evolve your career. When YOU thrive, WE thrive. Join Forces As a member of our dynamic team, you’ll tackle challenges and deliver digital services and solutions alongside supportive, inclusive and talented humans from within the team, and more broadly across the University. We are a powerful collective of diverse skillsets, strengths and experience and our practices promote an environment of collaboration where your unique contribution is welcomed and valued. When you join forces with Digital Services you join forces with Deakin. Visit Why Work at Deakin to view the overarching benefits of working with Deakin. Are you ready to make an impact with us? We value diversity and aim to build an inclusive environment that champions, embraces and respects differences. We support and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and people of all abilities, cultures, sexual orientation, and genders. Pre-employment safety and suitability checks: In accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence, appointment to this role is subject to successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, including Working With Children Checks and candidate gender-based violence declarations. Candidates may be asked to declare whether they have been investigated for an allegation of Gender‑based Violence, or determined to have engaged in conduct that constitutes Gender‑based Violence during the course of their previous employment, or otherwise in a legal process. A declaration is not required from individuals who have experienced gender‑based violence. A ‘Yes’ response does not automatically exclude a candidate from employment, and any information provided will be treated confidentially and considered only for relevance to the role and the University’s safety obligations. Advertised: 08 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 22 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/949/cw/en/job/589280/lead-foreign-engagement-compliance": {
      "title": "Lead, Foreign Engagement Compliance",
      "description": "Lead, Foreign Engagement Compliance Apply now Job no: 589280 Work type: Full-time Location: Flexible Categories: Legal Services, International Education Flexible location offer at Burwood or Geelong campuses + hybrid work arrangements Full-time and continuing – position to commence from January 2027 $143,627 - $152,229 (HEW 9) + 17% Superannuation About the team and role: Our Assurance capability, consisting of Legal Services, Compliance, Contract Governance and Risk develops the University’s assurance frameworks and policies, and provides key expertise that underpins compliance and corporate governance and Deakin's commitment to high standards of integrity. The Compliance and Contract Governance team ensures the organisation meets legal and regulatory obligations and manages a number of requirements including privacy, FOI, and declarations of interest. It also oversees contract governance to maintain transparency, mitigate risks, and uphold accountability in all contractual arrangements. As Lead, Foreign Engagement Compliance, you will be Deakin’s subject matter expert on foreign engagement obligations and associated regulatory requirements. You will provide strategic leadership and oversight of the University's foreign engagement compliance framework, ensuring Deakin can confidently pursue international partnerships, collaborative research and global engagement opportunities while meeting government expectations and legislative obligations. Working closely with stakeholders across research, international, legal, governance and operational functions, the role leads the development and implementation of an integrated, institution-wide approach to managing foreign engagement obligations and associated risks. The position ensures the University's activities align with legislative, regulatory and government requirements while supporting strategic research, international and partnership objectives. As a Lead, Foreign Engagement Compliance, you will: Lead and continuously improve the University’s foreign engagement compliance framework, ensuring alignment with legislative, regulatory and government requirements while supporting strategic research and international objectives. Provide expert advice on foreign engagement compliance, foreign interference risks and emerging regulatory obligations, translating complex legislation into practical policies, frameworks and controls. Develop, implement and continuously enhance governance, compliance and assurance processes for international collaborations, research partnerships, due diligence and regulatory reporting. Influence senior decision-making through strategic risk analysis, high-quality advice, governance reporting and engagement with stakeholders across academic and professional portfolios. Foster a culture of compliance and risk awareness by building organisational capability, leading assurance and continuous improvement initiatives, and strengthening institutional resilience and reputation. To be successful, you’ll have: Postgraduate qualifications and significant professional experience, or an equivalent combination of education, training and demonstrated expertise. Proven experience leading programs, functions or major initiatives within complex regulatory, compliance, governance, risk or assurance environments. Strong analytical and problem-solving skills, with the ability to interpret complex legislation, policy requirements and emerging regulatory issues to inform strategic outcomes. Demonstrated success influencing senior stakeholders, building strong relationships and providing trusted advice to support decision-making across large and complex organisations. Experience developing and implementing governance, compliance and assurance frameworks, ideally within higher education, research, government or other highly regulated sectors. Knowledge of foreign engagement, research security and related government policy requirements impacting the higher education and research sectors. Here's how to apply: Only people with the right to work in Australia may apply for this position. Please submit your updated resume and a short cover letter outlining your skills and experience by clicking ‘Apply Now’. For a confidential discussion regarding this role, please contact Nic Pappin (Head of Compliance & Contract Governance) on n.pappin@deakin.edu.au For a copy of the position description, please see below: Lead, Foreign Engagement Compliance_HEW 9.pdf Applications for this position close on Wednesday, 21 October 2026 at 11:55pm. Benefits for working with us! At Deakin, we offer more than just a job - we offer a lifestyle. Across our campuses in Geelong, Melbourne, and regional Victoria, you’ll find everything you need to thrive: on-site childcare, gyms, medical centres, cafes, accessible public transport, and convenient parking. Enjoy flexible working arrangements that support your work-life balance, generous leave entitlements including extended parental leave and the option to purchase additional time off, plus a wide range of staff discounts on travel and more. Our health and wellbeing services, ongoing professional development opportunities, and inclusive, supportive culture make Deakin a place where you can grow - whether you're working remotely or immersed in the vibrant energy of campus life. Are You Ready? Deakin is a Victorian university with a global impact. We are an agile, dynamic, and innovative university committed to making a positive impact through our excellence in education, research and innovation and the contributions we make to the wider community. We understand that our reputation has been built on the dedication and expertise of our staff and we offer a dynamic and diverse working environment with opportunities to grow and develop careers. We believe that a progressive, thriving culture will ensure that people choose to come, and stay at Deakin and contribute to our ongoing success. We value diversity and aim to build an inclusive environment that champions, embraces and respects differences. We support and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and people of all abilities, cultures, sexual orientation, and genders. Pre-employment safety and suitability checks: In accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence, appointment to this role is subject to successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, including Working With Children Checks and candidate gender-based violence declarations. Candidates may be asked to declare whether they have been investigated for an allegation of Gender‑based Violence, or determined to have engaged in conduct that constitutes Gender‑based Violence during the course of their previous employment, or otherwise in a legal process. A declaration is not required from individuals who have experienced gender‑based violence. A ‘Yes’ response does not automatically exclude a candidate from employment, and any information provided will be treated confidentially and considered only for relevance to the role and the University’s safety obligations. Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 21 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/949/cw/en/job/589594/coordinator-digital-technologies": {
      "title": "Coordinator, Digital Technologies",
      "description": "Coordinator, Digital Technologies Apply now Job no: 589594 Work type: Full-time Location: Melbourne - Burwood Categories: Technical Based at our Melbourne, Burwood Campus + hybrid work arrangements Full-time and continuing HEW 6 $100,128 - $108,130 + 17% Superannuation Why work with us? At Deakin you will have access to benefits such as a variety of leave options including generous parental leave and the ability to purchase additional leave; flexible working arrangements to help manage your work-life balance; ongoing learning and development opportunities to grow your career; an inclusive and supportive culture and environment to work in, both online and on campus. As a Digital Learning Technologies Coordinator, you will: Plan and prioritise work effectively, identifying barriers, monitoring progress and adapting approaches to achieve outcomes. Analyse information from multiple sources, identify key issues and develop practical solutions to support informed decision-making. Build productive stakeholder relationships, communicating clearly and providing confident, solutions-focused advice. Coordinate activities and projects to deliver quality outcomes within agreed timelines, priorities and constraints. Contribute to continuous improvement and team capability by seeking feedback, enhancing processes, sharing knowledge and supporting others. To be successful, you’ll have: Relevant degree with subsequent experience, or an equivalent combination of extensive experience, specialist knowledge, education and/or training. Demonstrated experience with professional film and television production equipment, including high-level camera systems such as ARRI, RED and Blackmagic Design, and sound, lighting and grip equipment. Experience using industry-standard post-production platforms, such as DaVinci Resolve, Pro Tools, Premiere Pro and Avid. Strong analytical, problem-solving and organisational skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and work effectively within relevant policies and governance frameworks. Strong stakeholder engagement and collaboration skills, with demonstrated experience working effectively across teams to achieve quality outcomes. Here's how to apply: Please submit your updated resume and a short cover letter outlining your skills and experience. For a confidential discussion regarding this role, please contact Thomas Salisbury (Senior Operations Coordinator) via email at thomas.salisbury@deakin.edu.au For a copy of the position description, please see below: #589594 - Coordinator, Digital Technologies HEW 6.pdf Applications for this position close on Wednesday 21 st October Are You Ready? Deakin is a Victorian university with a global impact. We are an agile, dynamic, and innovative university committed to making a positive impact through our excellence in education, research and innovation and the contributions we make to the wider community. We understand that our reputation has been built on the dedication and expertise of our staff and we offer a dynamic and diverse working environment with opportunities to grow and develop careers. We believe that a progressive, thriving culture will ensure that people choose to come, and stay at Deakin and contribute to our ongoing success. We value diversity and aim to build an inclusive environment that champions, embraces and respects differences. We support and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and people of all abilities, cultures, sexual orientation, and genders. Pre-employment safety and suitability checks: In accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence, appointment to this role is subject to successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, including Working With Children Checks and candidate gender-based violence declarations. Candidates may be asked to declare whether they have been investigated for an allegation of Gender‑based Violence, or determined to have engaged in conduct that constitutes Gender‑based Violence during the course of their previous employment, or otherwise in a legal process. A declaration is not required from individuals who have experienced gender‑based violence. A ‘Yes’ response does not automatically exclude a candidate from employment, and any information provided will be treated confidentially and considered only for relevance to the role and the University’s safety obligations. Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 21 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
