    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/949/cw/en/job/589710/officer-commonwealth-prac-payments": {
      "title": "Officer, Commonwealth Prac Payments",
      "description": "Officer, Commonwealth Prac Payments Apply now Job no: 589710 Work type: Full-time Location: Melbourne - Burwood Categories: Administration, Student Services Based at our Melbourne, Burwood Campus + hybrid work arrangements Full-time and fixed-term until 31 December 2027 HEW 5 $86,744 - $97,290 + 17% Superannuation Why work with us? At Deakin you will have access to benefits such as a variety of leave options including generous parental leave and the ability to purchase additional leave; flexible working arrangements to help manage your work-life balance; ongoing learning and development opportunities to grow your career; an inclusive and supportive culture and environment to work in, both online and on campus. As a Commonwealth Prac Payments officer, you will: Provide clear advice and guidance on Commonwealth Prac Payment eligibility and processes. Analyse information and communicate it effectively to support sound decision-making. Organise and prioritise work to successfully manage competing demands. Develop collaborative stakeholder relationships and contribute positively to team outcomes. Use sound judgement to resolve issues and improve processes and practices while delivering against service and funding requirements. To be successful, you’ll have: Relevant tertiary qualification with appropriate experience, or an equivalent combination of education, training and relevant experience. Demonstrated experience in providing advice and support, with strong communication and stakeholder engagement skills. Ability to interpret and apply procedures, guidelines and requirements accurately and consistently. Strong organisational skills, with the ability to coordinate activities, prioritise workloads and manage competing demands. Demonstrated analytical and problem-solving skills, with the ability to identify opportunities and contribute to process and service improvements. Here's how to apply: Please submit your updated resume and a short cover letter outlining your skills and experience. For a confidential discussion regarding this role, please contact Pamela Pritchard (Manager, Student Finance) via email at pamela.pritchard@deakin.edu.au For a copy of the position description, please see below: #589710 Officer, Commonwealth Prac Payments - HEW 5_PD.pdf Applications for this position close on Wednesday 21st October Are You Ready? Deakin is a Victorian university with a global impact. We are an agile, dynamic, and innovative university committed to making a positive impact through our excellence in education, research and innovation and the contributions we make to the wider community. We understand that our reputation has been built on the dedication and expertise of our staff and we offer a dynamic and diverse working environment with opportunities to grow and develop careers. We believe that a progressive, thriving culture will ensure that people choose to come, and stay at Deakin and contribute to our ongoing success. We value diversity and aim to build an inclusive environment that champions, embraces and respects differences. We support and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and people of all abilities, cultures, sexual orientation, and genders. Pre-employment safety and suitability checks: In accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence, appointment to this role is subject to successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, including Working With Children Checks and candidate gender-based violence declarations. Candidates may be asked to declare whether they have been investigated for an allegation of Gender‑based Violence, or determined to have engaged in conduct that constitutes Gender‑based Violence during the course of their previous employment, or otherwise in a legal process. A declaration is not required from individuals who have experienced gender‑based violence. A ‘Yes’ response does not automatically exclude a candidate from employment, and any information provided will be treated confidentially and considered only for relevance to the role and the University’s safety obligations. Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 21 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/949/cw/en/job/588291/senior-manager-research-platforms": {
      "title": "Senior Manager, Research Platforms",
      "description": "Senior Manager, Research Platforms Apply now Job no: 588291 Work type: Full-time Location: Geelong - City, Geelong - Waurn Ponds, Melbourne - Burwood, Melbourne - City Categories: Management, Information Technology Based at our Geelong or Burwood Campus + hybrid work arrangements Full-time and continuing HEW 10 $153,668 + 17% Superannuation Benefits for working with us! At Deakin, we offer more than just a job - we offer a lifestyle. Across our campuses in Geelong, Melbourne, and regional Victoria, you’ll find everything you need to thrive: on-site childcare, gyms, medical centres, cafes, accessible public transport, and convenient parking. Enjoy flexible working arrangements that support your work-life balance, generous leave entitlements including extended parental leave and the option to purchase additional time off, plus a wide range of staff discounts on travel and more. Our health and wellbeing services, ongoing professional development opportunities, and inclusive, supportive culture make Deakin a place where you can grow - whether you're working remotely or immersed in the vibrant energy of campus life. About the role Research at Deakin is enabled by a diverse and distributed ecosystem spanning specialist platforms, enterprise capabilities, locally managed solutions, shared services and external partners. Sitting within Digital's Systems & Solutions team, you'll be accountable for the day-to-day delivery, support, performance and lifecycle of a focused set of Research applications and services. Beyond these direct responsibilities, you’ll play a broader role in shaping how Digital supports Research, helping to improve the consistency, effectiveness and sustainability of digital capabilities and services across the University. Partnering with our faculties, institutes and portfolios, a key focus will be building a clearer picture of the technology capabilities and services that enable Research. Through this process, governance and shared approaches will be strengthened, while ensuring specialised research needs are identified and supported appropriately. The objective isn’t to centralise every capability within Digital. It’s to make the distributed ecosystem work more effectively by improving visibility, strengthening ownership and lifecycle accountability. There isn't a fully defined roadmap waiting for you. You'll need the curiosity and initiative to understand the landscape, build trusted relationships and turn ambiguity into a practical direction for improvement. While leading a small team, much of your impact will come through engagement, collaboration and influence across a much broader university community. As the Senior Manager, Research Platforms, you will: Lead the delivery and operation of Digital's Research platforms and services , ensuring they are effectively supported, maintained, secure, reliable and continuously improved. Create greater visibility and cohesion across the Research technology ecosystem, its capabilities, services, dependencies and ownership. Connect Research with Digital capability , identifying where shared services and established capabilities can better enable Research. Strengthen governance and accountability , creating practical ownership and lifecycle models across a distributed environment. Turn ambiguity into direction and action , establishing priorities and driving meaningful service and capability improvements. Build stronger partnerships between Research and Digital , bringing different needs and perspectives together while influencing priorities, investment and decisions across the broader Research technology landscape. About you You’re an experienced Digital/IT services leader who combines sound judgement with a pragmatic and collaborative approach. Your leadership style enables you to develop teams, work effectively with senior stakeholders and specialists, and build credibility across different areas of an organisation. Experience within higher education would be highly regarded. Direct Research technology leadership is valuable, but it isn’t the only path to this role. Equally important is a strong understanding of digital services, genuine interest in Research and the researcher experience, and the curiosity to learn and understand a specialised environment. You’ll also bring relevant postgraduate qualifications and/or extensive relevant professional experience. Help us understand why this opportunity is the right fit for you Along with your CV, please include a brief, tailored cover letter telling us: what draws you to the intersection of Research and Digital; how your experience relates to the challenge we've described; and what you would bring to creating greater clarity, cohesion and direction across the Research technology landscape. We'd value a concise and considered explanation of why this particular opportunity resonates with you and what you believe you could contribute. For a confidential discussion regarding this role, please contact Kevin Brown (Associate Director, Business Systems) on kevin.brown@deakin.edu.au For a copy of the position description, please see below: Senior Manager Research Platforms - HEW 10_PD.pdf Applications for this position close on Sunday 18th October, 11:55pm. Previous applicants need not apply. Are You Ready? Deakin is a Victorian university with a global impact. We are an agile, dynamic, and innovative university committed to making a positive impact through our excellence in education, research and innovation and the contributions we make to the wider community. We understand that our reputation has been built on the dedication and expertise of our staff and we offer a dynamic and diverse working environment with opportunities to grow and develop careers. We believe that a progressive, thriving culture will ensure that people choose to come, and stay at Deakin and contribute to our ongoing success. We value diversity and aim to build an inclusive environment that champions, embraces and respects differences. We support and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and people of all abilities, cultures, sexual orientation, and genders. Pre-employment safety and suitability checks: In accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence, appointment to this role is subject to successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, including Working With Children Checks and candidate gender-based violence declarations. Candidates may be asked to declare whether they have been investigated for an allegation of Gender‑based Violence, or determined to have engaged in conduct that constitutes Gender‑based Violence during the course of their previous employment, or otherwise in a legal process. A declaration is not required from individuals who have experienced gender‑based violence. A ‘Yes’ response does not automatically exclude a candidate from employment, and any information provided will be treated confidentially and considered only for relevance to the role and the University’s safety obligations. Advertised: 05 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/949/cw/en/job/589692/associate-research-fellow": {
      "title": "Associate Research Fellow",
      "description": "Associate Research Fellow Apply now Job no: 589692 Work type: Full-time Location: Geelong - Waurn Ponds Categories: Research, Education, Engineering, Science Based at our Geelong, Waurn Ponds Campus + hybrid work arrangements Full-time (Part-time arrangements available) and fixed-term until December 2028 $86,599 - $116,417 (Level A) + 17% superannuation Why you will love this Role: Here is an exciting opportunity to work on meaningful research and contribute to making mineral processing more sustainable and efficient. Work with leading researchers and PhD students and learn from their expertise and be part of a collaborative research team. Strengthen and develop new skills, share ideas and grow as a researcher. Make a difference through research that benefits Deakin and the wider community. What you will Deliver: High-quality research and creative activities that generate impactful publications, outputs and contributions to the field. Collaboration across disciplines and with industry partners, building strong research networks and identifying opportunities for partnerships. Research funding capability and leading competitive grant and funding applications. Support and mentorship to students and early-career researchers, including co-supervising Honours/HDR students and working with experienced mentors. A strong research profile and impact, communicating outcomes effectively and developing a track record of excellence, timely delivery, recognition and community/industry impact. You may be a great fit for this role if you have: PhD and/or other relevant qualifications and experience in chemical engineering, materials engineering/science, environmental engineering or related technical disciplines. Demonstrated experience with particle agglomeration and laboratory proficiency. Emerging research and scholarship through publications, and/or exhibitions as appropriate to the discipline. Capacity to contribute to teaching, research and administration. Excellent interpersonal skills and a proven ability to establish good working relationships with colleagues. Ability to contribute to communities and engagement through research. This position may require the incumbent to travel domestically and/or internationally to attend conferences, events and to represent the university. Please note that only candidates with full working rights in Australia will be considered for this position. Here's how to apply: Please submit your current resume and a short cover letter outlining your skills and experience and in addition, address the Key Selection Criteria. Want to Know More? For a confidential discussion, or for further questions regarding this role, please contact Ellen Moon, Associate Professor via ellen.moon@deakin.edu.au For a copy of the position description, please see below: Associate Research Fellow_Minerals Processing.pdf Applications for this position close at 11.55pm on 18 October 2026. Please note that only candidates with full working rights in Australia will be considered for this position. Discover Deakin At Deakin , our fundamental role is delivering outcomes that benefit society through exploring and developing great ideas. All efforts across our education and research portfolios are focused on achieving this endeavour as we collaborate with others to effect ‘local change that matters beyond the local’. As Australia's largest university excelling in both education and research, we are committed to upholding the highest academic standards while addressing the broader challenges facing society. Deakin is committed to maintaining the social licence of universities – demonstrating the value we create for our communities. We're dedicated to building trust through legitimacy, credibility and accountability. What we Offer At Deakin , you'll be supported to thrive both professionally and personally. We offer flexible working arrangements, competitive salary and superannuation benefits, wellbeing initiatives, generous leave provisions, and professional development opportunities to help you achieve your career goals. Join a collaborative and inclusive community that values innovation, flexibility, diversity, and making a positive impact through education, research, and engagement. To find out why our staff love working with Deakin, read more here ! Pre-employment safety and suitability checks: In accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence, appointment to this role is subject to successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, including Working with Children Checks and candidate gender-based violence declarations. Any information provided will be treated confidentially and assessed only where relevant to the requirements of the role and Deakin's duty of care obligations. A declaration does not automatically preclude a candidate from employment. Advertised: 02 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 18 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/949/cw/en/job/589671/senior-officer-performance-and-insights-reporting": {
      "title": "Senior Officer, Performance and Insights Reporting",
      "description": "Senior Officer, Performance and Insights Reporting Apply now Job no: 589671 Work type: Part-time Location: Flexible Categories: Administration, Technical Flexible location + hybrid work arrangements Part-time (0.8 FTE) and fixed-term until October 2028 $100,128-$108,130 (HEW 6) + 17% Superannuation Why this role matters As the Senior Officer, Performance and Insights Reporting, you will help drive evidence-informed decision-making across the Institute for Health Transformation (IHT) and the Western Victorian Primary Health Network (WVPHN). By transforming complex data into clear, actionable insights, you will support organisational performance, strategic planning and continuous improvement initiatives that contribute to better health outcomes for communities across Western Victoria. Collaborating with stakeholders across research, health and operational settings, you will develop meaningful reporting, monitor key performance measures and identify emerging trends and opportunities. Your ability to communicate insights effectively will support leaders to make informed decisions, evaluate progress against strategic objectives and respond to evolving priorities. This role offers the opportunity to influence how evidence is used to guide action, strengthen organisational capability and enhance the impact of health initiatives. Through your expertise in reporting, analysis and stakeholder engagement, you will contribute to a culture that values data-driven insights, accountability and continuous improvement. As a Senior Officer, Performance and Insights Reporting you will: Deliver performance reporting, analytics and insights that support evidence-based decision-making and strategic priorities. Analyse and interpret complex data to identify trends, measure outcomes and inform organisational performance. Develop and enhance reporting frameworks, dashboards and performance measures that drive accountability and continuous improvement. Collaborate with stakeholders to understand reporting requirements and translate findings into meaningful, actionable insights. Prepare high-quality reports and presentations that support governance, planning and decision-making across the Western Victorian Primary Health Network and the Institute for Health Transformation. To be successful, you’ll have: Demonstrated experience in performance reporting, analytics, business intelligence or related disciplines, with the ability to translate complex data into meaningful insights. Strong analytical and problem-solving skills, including experience interpreting data, identifying trends and informing evidence-based decision-making. Experience developing reports, dashboards, performance measures and reporting frameworks that support organisational objectives and continuous improvement. Proven ability to build effective stakeholder relationships and communicate insights clearly through high-quality reports, briefings and presentations. An understanding of healthcare, public health, research, evaluation or related sectors, with an appreciation for how data and insights can inform strategy, performance and outcomes. Here's how to apply: Please submit your updated resume and a short cover letter outlining your skills and experience. For any confidential enquiries regarding this role, please email Madison Frith (Associate Research Fellow) on madison.frith@deakin.edu.au For a copy of the position description, please see below: PD - Senior Officer, Performance and Insights Reporting_HEW6.pdf Applications for this position close on Thursday 15 th October at 11:55pm. Benefits for working with us! At Deakin, we offer more than just a job. We offer a rewarding career in a flexible, inclusive and forward-thinking environment. Across our campuses in Geelong, Melbourne and regional Victoria, you'll enjoy flexible working arrangements that support work-life balance, generous leave provisions including extended parental leave and the option to purchase additional leave, and access to a range of professional development opportunities to help you grow your career. Are You Ready? Deakin is a Victorian university with a global impact. We are an agile, dynamic, and innovative university committed to making a positive impact through our excellence in education, research and innovation and the contributions we make to the wider community. We understand that our reputation has been built on the dedication and expertise of our staff and we offer a dynamic and diverse working environment with opportunities to grow and develop careers. We believe that a progressive, thriving culture will ensure that people choose to come, and stay at Deakin and contribute to our ongoing success. We value diversity and aim to build an inclusive environment that champions, embraces and respects differences. We support and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and people of all abilities, cultures, sexual orientation, and genders. Pre-employment safety and suitability checks: In accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence, appointment to this role is subject to successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, including Working With Children Checks and candidate gender-based violence declarations. Candidates may be asked to declare whether they have been investigated for an allegation of Gender‑based Violence, or determined to have engaged in conduct that constitutes Gender‑based Violence during the course of their previous employment, or otherwise in a legal process. A declaration is not required from individuals who have experienced gender‑based violence. A ‘Yes’ response does not automatically exclude a candidate from employment, and any information provided will be treated confidentially and considered only for relevance to the role and the University’s safety obligations. Advertised: 01 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://careers.pageuppeople.com/949/cw/en/job/589124/coordinator-content-marketing": {
      "title": "Coordinator, Content Marketing",
      "description": "Coordinator, Content Marketing Apply now Job no: 589124 Work type: Full-time Location: Geelong - City, Melbourne - Burwood Categories: Marketing and Communications Based at our Melbourne Burwood or Geelong Waterfront Campus + hybrid work arrangements Full-time and continuing HEW 7 $110,171 - $121,309 + 17% Superannuation About the role: The Coordinator, Content Marketing plays an important role in developing and delivering engaging, audience-focused content and communications across Deakin’s channels. Working across a diverse mix of projects, the role helps strengthen Deakin’s brand, connect with key audiences and support broader marketing priorities. The role contributes to the ongoing evolution of Deakin’s Content Hub, supports the delivery of recruitment publications and contributes to communications planning and the development of campaign and advertising content. There is plenty of variety, with opportunities to take content from idea through to delivery and find creative ways to bring Deakin’s stories, expertise and experience to life. Working closely with colleagues and stakeholders across the University, the Coordinator uses strong content expertise, audience insight and sound judgment to turn complex information into clear, engaging and brand aligned communications. We’re looking for a proactive, organised content professional who enjoys building strong relationships, leading projects and delivering great work in a dynamic environment. As a Coordinator, Content Marketing, you will: Lead and coordinate content projects from concept to delivery. Analyse content and performance data to provide insights to support decision-making. Identify opportunities for improvement and implement change across content and communications activities. Build and maintain strong stakeholder relationships delivering practical solutions that meet business needs. Support capability development through constructive feedback and knowledge sharing. To be successful, you’ll have: Degree with substantial subsequent experience; and or extensive experience management expertise in technical or administrative fields; or equivalent combination of experience and/or education/training. Experience coordinating, leading and delivering complex work. Knowledge of content marketing principles, editorial standards, and user-centred communication strategies, particularly in a higher education or alarge organisational context. Excellent organisational and project management skills, with the ability to work independently, manage competing priorities, and meet deadlines. A solid understanding of SEO and AEO, with experience supporting or delivering training for writers and contributors. Here's how to apply: Please submit your updated resume and a short cover letter outlining your skills and experience. For a confidential discussion regarding this role, please contact Sinead Pearson, Manager, Marketing Communications on sinead.pearson@deakin.edu.au For a copy of the position description, please see below: PD - Coordinator Content Marketing 589124.pdf Applications for this position close on 15 Oct 2026 Why work with us? At Deakin you will have access to benefits such as a variety of leave options including generous parental leave and the ability to purchase additional leave; flexible working arrangements to help manage your work-life balance; ongoing learning and development opportunities to grow your career; an inclusive and supportive culture and environment to work in, both online and on campus. Are You Ready? Deakin is a Victorian university with a global impact. We are an agile, dynamic, and innovative university committed to making a positive impact through our excellence in education, research and innovation and the contributions we make to the wider community. We understand that our reputation has been built on the dedication and expertise of our staff and we offer a dynamic and diverse working environment with opportunities to grow and develop careers. We believe that a progressive, thriving culture will ensure that people choose to come, and stay at Deakin and contribute to our ongoing success. We value diversity and aim to build an inclusive environment that champions, embraces and respects differences. We support and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, and people of all abilities, cultures, sexual orientation, and genders. Pre-employment safety and suitability checks: In accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence, appointment to this role is subject to successful completion of relevant pre-employment checks, including Working With Children Checks and candidate gender-based violence declarations. Candidates may be asked to declare whether they have been investigated for an allegation of Gender‑based Violence, or determined to have engaged in conduct that constitutes Gender‑based Violence during the course of their previous employment, or otherwise in a legal process. A declaration is not required from individuals who have experienced gender‑based violence. A ‘Yes’ response does not automatically exclude a candidate from employment, and any information provided will be treated confidentially and considered only for relevance to the role and the University’s safety obligations. Advertised: 01 Oct 2026 AUS Eastern Standard Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
